{#
  IP SLA active probes for Cisco IOS / iol — a Twinlab built-in custom-config
  template (netlab models no active measurement). Rendered per node over the
  netlab data model (referenced as `config: [ip_sla]`).

  On each PE (a node with the customer VPN), define one ICMP-echo probe per
  remote customer site: sourced from this PE's VRF loopback, targeting every other
  PE's VRF loopback inside vrf vpn_a — proving the L3VPN data path end to end. The
  probe number is the remote site's host octet (stable, collision-free); the probe
  runs at the IOS default 60s cadence. Probes are scheduled to run forever.

  `vrf` is a sub-command of the icmp-echo *operation* (IOS config-ip-sla-echo
  mode), one level below `icmp-echo`, so it is indented two spaces — ios_config
  uses indentation to re-enter the right mode and to match the running config.
#}
{% if mpls.vpn is defined and vrfs is defined and 'vpn_a' in vrfs %}
{% set src = vrfs.vpn_a.loopback_address.ipv4.split('/')[0] %}
{% for nb in bgp.neighbors|default([]) if nb.vpnv4 is defined %}
{% set dst = hostvars[nb.name].vrfs.vpn_a.loopback_address.ipv4.split('/')[0] %}
ip sla {{ dst.split('.')[-1] }}
 icmp-echo {{ dst }} source-ip {{ src }}
  vrf vpn_a
ip sla schedule {{ dst.split('.')[-1] }} life forever start-time now
!
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
